WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.690
Countless kids grew up with A Christmas Story,
and have gone from watching it with youthful

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.500
wonder to seeing it through older, wiser eyes.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.929
In celebration of that inevitable shift in
perception, here's a look at things only adults

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:14.510
notice in A Christmas Story.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:18.780
One of the hallmarks of A Christmas Story
is the rich fantasy life that Ralphie indulges

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.340
in on his road to Christmas Day and, hopefully,
his Red Ryder BB gun.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.150
The most elaborate of these fantasies involves
him imagining what he might use the gun for,

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.340
as bandits led by Black Bart attack the family
home, forcing Ralphie and his trusty new weapon

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.070
to defend his family.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.160
It plays out like the perfect kid fantasy
of a Western in their very own neighborhood,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.870
but it takes a slightly dark turn when, at
the end, it's pretty clear that Ralphie has

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.829
killed Black Bart's gang.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:49.030
Kids might not notice this, but the lolling
tongues and crossed out eyes suggest death,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.040
and he was, after all, shooting them.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.670
Yes, it's just a fantasy, but it's a grim
one for a kid who mostly just wants to shoot

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.130
targets in the backyard.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:03.040
Ralphie is nine years old as most of A Christmas
Story unfolds, placing him in a rather interesting

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.420
in-between age.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.490
He's young enough to still consider things
like Christmas and Santa Claus the most important

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.780
parts of his life, and to care deeply about
things like Little Orphan Annie secret decoder

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.830
rings, but he's also old enough to fancy himself
more mature than his younger brother.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.020
It's subtle, but this budding maturity comes
out in a couple of key moments when it becomes

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.880
clear that Ralphie is becoming interested
in women.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.830
When The Old Man sets up the leg lamp for
the first time, Ralphie basically cops a feel

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:34.610
of the thing, and in both his fantasies and
in real life it's clear that he at the very

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.950
least wants his teacher to like him.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.570
He might even have a little crush on Miss
Shields.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:45.020
Ralphie's quest for his Red Ryder BB gun culminates
when, feeling all of the adults in his life

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.100
have forsaken him, he decides to appeal directly
to Santa at the local department store on

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.299
Christmas Eve.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:56.070
This backfires spectacularly, as Ralphie spends
the entire time waiting in line trying to

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.479
focus on his goal, only to forget everything
he was going to say when he finally climbs

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:02.470
the towering North Pole structure.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:07.080
To make matters worse, the Santa at the top
seems unusually intimidating and even frightening

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.580
to Ralphie's eyes, as he panics amid a closing
store and a group of pushy elves.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.080
It's clear that Ralphie blew his shot, but
what's less clear is why.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.020
What about his past history with Santa made
him so nervous about asking for something

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.020
from the big guy?

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.370
Was he just psyched out by all of the rejections
that came before, or is it more specific?

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.950
A Christmas Story is a tale of kid concerns
and kid perceptions, so everything in Ralphie's

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.390
world is just a little bit heightened.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.590
This includes his encounters with the bully
Scut Farkus, who seems to lurk around every

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:42.260
corner and takes his sick delight in the pain
of other kids to near supervillain levels,

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.160
at least until Ralphie finally finds it within
himself to fight back.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.230
To any kid watching, bullying is, if not a
reality, at least a familiar abstract concept

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.710
they've had to deal with in their lives, so
they may simply accept it and move on with

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:55.710
the story.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:00.050
For adults, though, there's a sense that the
bullying in A Christmas Story is particularly

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:04.440
dark in part because the adults surrounding
the children don't really do that much about

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.030
it, and the kids don't necessarily want them
to.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:11.010
Farkus haunts Ralphie and his friends all
the time, but they don't tell on him.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.393
Flick walks into class with a black eye one
day, and Miss Shields pays little attention to it.

00:03:15.647 --> 00:03:19.590
Even when Ralphie finally beats the hell out
of Farkus, his mother ignores the other kid

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.989
in the fight and focuses only on her son,
never really stopping to ask what happens.

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:28.630
It calls to mind a world in which kids are
entirely on their own, and to modern audiences

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.685
that can be a little scary.

00:03:30.685 --> 00:03:35.170
Ralphie's father, only ever referred to as
The Old Man, is a typical representation of

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:38.170
a Midwestern father figure in the early 20th
century.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:43.840
He's stern but not cruel, caring but not affectionate,
and distant but not absent.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.739
He's a man's man, and he spends most of the
film just trying to read his newspaper and

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:51.500
enjoy a little bit of turkey while his sons
get into trouble around him.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.246
If you look close enough, though, there are
little hints of eccentricity in The Old Man's life.

00:03:55.461 --> 00:03:59.730
He insists on being timed when he changes
a tire, for example, and went through the

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.959
trouble of burying his "major award" in the
backyard and supposedly playing "Taps" in

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:06.569
tribute to it as he did.

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:10.952
He even takes some time in the department
store to skip along with the Wizard of Oz characters.

00:04:11.148 --> 00:04:16.042
Ralphie doesn't think much about it, but there's
more to his Old Man than newspapers and furnace battles.

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:20.419
He has a rich inner life that we only see
glimpses of.

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:24.000
Despite being a ubiquitous movie, there are
still things about A Christmas Story that

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.880
some viewers can't agree on.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.020
A key point of contention comes midway through,
when the Old Man's beloved "major award" is

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.900
broken, and he's unable to glue it back together.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.680
The mother readily admits to breaking the
leg lamp, but claims it was an accident that

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.139
happened while she was watering plants.

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:42.620
Some viewers believe her, while others think
that she did it deliberately because she was

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:47.350
sick of looking at the thing, and indeed she
does confirm she thinks it's

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:51.935
"the ugliest lamp I have ever seen in my entire
life"

00:04:51.935 --> 00:04:53.340
So what really happened?

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.039
Look at the expression on her face just as
she heads into the front room with her watering

00:04:57.039 --> 00:05:01.710
can, knowing she will be alone with the lamp
for a moment, and listen to the sheer force

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:02.901
of the breaking sound.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.339
She definitely meant to do it.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:10.849
These days we think of Christmas as a season
that begins roughly at the end of Thanksgiving,

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:15.779
a period of weeks packed with decorations,
shopping, seasonal programming, and all manner

00:05:15.779 --> 00:05:17.499
of parties and other events.

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:20.970
The way Ralphie obsesses over Christmas in
the film, you'd think that it would be similar

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:26.160
for him, but in the 1940-ish era in which
the film takes place, that's just not the case.

00:05:26.296 --> 00:05:30.860
For the Parker family, Christmas as an observable
holiday really only lasts a couple of days.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.780
They go and get the Christmas tree, then Christmas
Eve arrives shortly thereafter and the family

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.389
packs in a parade, shopping, decorating, and
visits to Santa all in one night.

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:43.580
There is no Black Friday, no long period of
shipping gifts to the front door, no early

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.610
December Christmas parades and no long slog
of Christmas parties to march through on the

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.069
way to the big day.

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:54.979
The Parkers basically wait until Christmas
as a day to arrive, and then celebrate.

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:59.409
It's not a huge plot point, and kids might
not notice it, but adults whose entire schedules

00:05:59.409 --> 00:06:03.090
are thrown out of whack in December definitely
do.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.719
The leg lamp in A Christmas Story has become
so iconic that it's almost more famous than

00:06:07.719 --> 00:06:12.229
any of the film's actual human characters,
but until it arrives at the Parker residence,

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:13.440
it could be anything.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.110
The Old Man comes home from work one day,
says he's going to be getting "a major award"

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.669
that every night, and then a crate shows up.

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:20.960
"Ah, frah-gee-lay.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.530
It must be Italian!"

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:27.399
"I think that says fragile, honey."

00:06:27.399 --> 00:06:28.689
"Oh, yeah."

00:06:28.689 --> 00:06:32.659
Before it does, though, the Old Man is just
musing aloud about what the award might be,

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:37.710
and even dares to think that someone might
be sending him the deed to his very own bowling alley.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:42.009
This raises a couple of questions: What does
the Old Man do for a living, and what on Earth

00:06:42.009 --> 00:06:46.529
did he do to earn a major award that could
range from a weird novelty lamp all the way

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:48.539
to a piece of commercial property?

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:53.289
We don't learn much about the Old Man's work,
and the fluidity of this moment and what exactly

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:57.929
might have happened just further underlines
a common kid truth: They often really don't

00:06:57.929 --> 00:06:59.478
know what their parents do all day.

00:07:00.322 --> 00:07:04.689
When the hard Midwest winter comes in A Christmas
Story, Ralphie's mother bundles up Randy in

00:07:04.689 --> 00:07:09.639
such a way that Ralphie even compares it to
deep sea diving, and Randy famously complains

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:12.899
that he's wearing so many layers that he can't
put his arms down.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:17.319
Once outside, Randy can't even get up off
the ground by himself because his movement

00:07:17.319 --> 00:07:18.740
is so hampered.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:22.309
It's comical, but it also makes you think
of what exactly was supposed to happen when

00:07:22.309 --> 00:07:23.720
he got to school.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.756
Randy couldn't very well pull all of that
stuff off himself, so who was doing it for him?

00:07:27.756 --> 00:07:31.943
More importantly, who was re-bundling him
when it was time to go home for the day?

00:07:31.943 --> 00:07:35.840
Somewhere in that school there's a poor employee
who had to be on Randy Parker Winter Wear

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.249
duty day after day, and they very likely weren't
happy about it.

00:07:40.249 --> 00:07:43.699
The Old Man in A Christmas Story has many
different nemeses.

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:47.339
There's the furnace that won't stop acting
up in the basement, the Oldsmobile that won't

00:07:47.339 --> 00:07:49.930
run right, and of course, the dogs next door.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.759
We know very little about the Bumpus family
in the house beside the Parkers.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:57.739
All we really know is that they have a lot
of dogs and those dogs really hate the Old

00:07:57.739 --> 00:08:00.009
Man in an almost pathological way.

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:04.139
They follow him from his car to his door,
try to push their way into the house, and

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:08.520
ultimately devour the family's turkey when
the door is left open just enough for them

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.704
to get through and wreak havoc.

00:08:10.704 --> 00:08:14.539
Dogs will be dogs, of course, but the film
doesn't tell us what else is going on that

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:16.119
allows this to happen.

00:08:16.119 --> 00:08:19.479
Why hasn't the old man marched over and had
it out with Mr. Bumpus?

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:23.449
Of course, the plot answer is so he still
has to do battle with the dogs, but it's hard

00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:27.090
not to wonder how things got this bad in the
first place.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.339
A Christmas Story is a film that depends in
many ways upon seeing the world from Ralphie's

00:08:31.339 --> 00:08:35.370
point of view, which means that our perspective
on the various other members of the family

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.810
is slightly skewed, and emphasizes Ralphie's
father more than his mother.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:43.959
Frequent rewatching of the film reveals, especially
to an adult audience, that Mrs. Parker is

00:08:43.959 --> 00:08:48.750
in fact a much bigger part of Ralphie's emotional
memory than we give her credit for.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:53.190
Yes, she's there for him after his fight with
Farkus, but she's also a tender presence in

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.300
other ways.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.430
She leads the silly Christmas carols in the
car, has an innate understanding of how Randy's

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:02.810
mind works, proves she has the most patience
of anyone in the family when it counts, and

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:07.620
even reveals a moment of real tenderness with
her husband in the film's final scene.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.730
Mrs. Parker is much more than a put-upon wife
and mother within this story.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:16.230
She has, in many ways, an inner life that's
just as rich as her husband's.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.899
One of the most famous scenes in A Christmas
Story is the moment when Ralphie makes a mistake

00:09:19.899 --> 00:09:22.545
and utters the curse which the movie renders
as

00:09:22.545 --> 00:09:28.913
"Oh, Fudge."

00:09:28.913 --> 00:09:32.660
This moment, and the resulting fallout, is
mostly about Ralphie getting punished for

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:36.440
cursing in the first place, and then blaming
it on his friend, something a lot of kids

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:37.910
have no doubt done.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.509
But while the kids are busy relating to the
punishment phase of that story, the adults

00:09:41.509 --> 00:09:45.819
are probably paying a little more attention
to the way Ralphie's mother reacts.

00:09:45.819 --> 00:09:50.300
A Christmas Story is a PG movie, so we don't
hear too many curses thrown around, but Ralphie

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:54.540
makes a point of telling the audience that
he's heard that particular bad word frequently

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.123
from his father, something Schwartz's mother
even guesses over the phone.

00:09:58.123 --> 00:10:02.880
We as an audience also hear Mr. Parker's nonsensical
cursing wafting up from the furnace throughout

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.545
the film, so we can guess at what else he
might say in other situations.

00:10:06.545 --> 00:10:11.260
Why, then, does Mrs. Parker seem to have no
clue as to the kind of words her husband might

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:12.943
use in a high-stress moment?

00:10:12.943 --> 00:10:17.000
Whatever's going on there, this family has
an unhealthy relationship with profanity,

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.790
and they need to all get on the same page.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.899
